{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>And You said, I will surely deal well with you.</b> Even though his fear was lest sin cause [harm], he said: You have done great acts of kindness with me that I was not worthy of — all the more so should You do this kindness with me gratuitously, which You promised me that You would deal well with me and multiply my seed. And my sin is not worthy of preventing the goodness that You promised me, for even from the beginning I was not worthy of it if You were to keep account of iniquities, and You did not promise it to me on account of my deeds but only because of Your abundant kindnesses. Some say that Jacob had pity on his sons and his household lest Esau strike them, for he did not know whether the promise 'and your seed shall be as the dust of the earth' referred to these or to others, for it is possible that he alone might escape and afterward multiply his seed. This is not correct in my eyes, for if that were his thought, how could he say in his prayer, 'And You said, I will surely deal well with you and I will set your seed'? Moreover, there he was told, 'Behold, I am with you and will keep you wherever you go and will bring you back to this land, for I will not forsake you,' and if his sons were to fall by the sword of his brother, this promise would not be fulfilled. Therefore he hints by saying 'I will surely deal well with you' and likewise 'I will deal well with you,' for all this follows from what was told to him (Genesis 31:3), 'I will be with you.' But everything is from the fear of sin, for the way of the righteous is to be in constant awe, and he feared lest perhaps even after leaving there he had sinned by entering a covenant with Laban, an idol worshiper, or in some other matter — and who can understand his errors?"], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:38:42.023606096Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}